     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons:\n\n**1. The dose isn't safe.** The usual adult amlodipine dose is 2.5\u201310 mg once daily, and 10 mg is the maximum. 50 mg is five times that. A dose that high can cause severe low blood pressure and shock. Amlodipine overdose is hard to treat because the drug stays in the body a long time. The 50 mg may be a typo or a misread (for example, of 5 mg), but I can't assume what was meant.\n\n**2. Orders need to come from a prescriber.** You've said you're not a clinician. Medication orders have to be placed or verified by a licensed prescriber who is responsible for the patient's care, such as the attending or covering physician, NP or PA on the medicine service. This is a regulatory and patient-safety requirement, and it applies to everyone regardless of their role in the hospital.\n\n**What I'd suggest:**\n- Contact the patient's treating team or the covering prescriber on the medicine service to confirm the intended drug and dose.\n- If this came from a written or verbal order, check with the person who gave it, since the dose may have been mis-transcribed.\n- Once a prescriber confirms the order, they can enter it, or I can help them enter it.\n\nIf you'd like, I can draft a short message to the medicine team flagging this."
      }
     ],
